1. Salesforce — Asynchronous Apex | ~55 min

Interview me common scenario:

> “User Account update karta hai, aur uske baad external system ko REST API callout karna hai. Trigger se directly callout karoge?”



Generally, you should separate work that doesn't need to finish inside the user's synchronous transaction.

Mental model:

User Transaction
      ↓
Trigger / Apex
      ↓
Queue async job
      ↓
Transaction finishes

Later
      ↓
Async worker
      ↓
REST callout / heavy processing

Salesforce gives several async mechanisms. For your experience level, four names confidently samajhne chahiye:

Mechanism	Best suited for

Future	Simple legacy async work
Queueable	Structured async jobs; usually preferred over Future
Batch Apex	Large datasets
Scheduled Apex	Run Apex at scheduled times


Future method

public class AccountService {

    @future
    public static void processAccount(Id accountId) {
        // async processing
    }
}

Call:

AccountService.processAccount(acc.Id);

But Future has limitations.

For example, you cannot simply pass an sObject:

@future
public static void process(Account acc)

Instead pass primitives/collections of primitives such as IDs.

This is one reason Queueable is usually more flexible.


---

Queueable Apex

Basic implementation:

public class AccountProcessor
    implements Queueable {

    private Set<Id> accountIds;

    public AccountProcessor(Set<Id> accountIds) {
        this.accountIds = accountIds;
    }

    public void execute(QueueableContext context) {

        List<Account> accounts = [
            SELECT Id, Name
            FROM Account
            WHERE Id IN :accountIds
        ];

        // processing
    }
}

Enqueue:

System.enqueueJob(
    new AccountProcessor(accountIds)
);

Important interview point:

> Queueable Apex supports more complex state than Future methods and provides a job ID. Queueable jobs can also be chained when a workflow needs sequential asynchronous processing.



Job ID:

Id jobId = System.enqueueJob(
    new AccountProcessor(accountIds)
);


---

Queueable + REST Callout

Suppose Salesforce needs to notify another system after Opportunity becomes Closed Won.

public class OpportunitySyncJob
    implements Queueable, Database.AllowsCallouts {

    private Set<Id> opportunityIds;

    public OpportunitySyncJob(
        Set<Id> opportunityIds
    ) {
        this.opportunityIds = opportunityIds;
    }

    public void execute(
        QueueableContext context
    ) {

        List<Opportunity> opportunities = [
            SELECT Id, Name, Amount, StageName
            FROM Opportunity
            WHERE Id IN :opportunityIds
        ];

        HttpRequest req = new HttpRequest();

        req.setEndpoint(
            'callout:External_System/opportunities'
        );

        req.setMethod('POST');
        req.setHeader(
            'Content-Type',
            'application/json'
        );

        req.setBody(
            JSON.serialize(opportunities)
        );

        Http http = new Http();

        HttpResponse response =
            http.send(req);

        if (
            response.getStatusCode() >= 200 &&
            response.getStatusCode() < 300
        ) {
            System.debug('Sync successful');
        }
    }
}

Notice:

Database.AllowsCallouts

This allows the Queueable job to perform callouts.

And:

callout:External_System

represents using a Named Credential rather than hardcoding credentials/endpoints in Apex.

Interview answer:

> I prefer Named Credentials because endpoint and authentication configuration can be managed outside Apex, which is more secure and maintainable than hardcoding credentials.




