Batch Apex

Imagine:

> Process 2 million Accounts.



Trying:

List<Account> accounts = [
    SELECT Id
    FROM Account
];

inside a normal synchronous transaction is obviously not the design.

Batch Apex divides large processing into chunks.

Structure:

public class AccountBatch
    implements Database.Batchable<SObject> {

    public Database.QueryLocator start(
        Database.BatchableContext bc
    ) {

        return Database.getQueryLocator([
            SELECT Id, Status__c
            FROM Account
        ]);
    }

    public void execute(
        Database.BatchableContext bc,
        List<Account> scope
    ) {

        for (Account acc : scope) {
            acc.Status__c = 'Reviewed';
        }

        update scope;
    }

    public void finish(
        Database.BatchableContext bc
    ) {
        // post-processing
    }
}

Run:

Database.executeBatch(
    new AccountBatch(),
    200
);

Conceptually:

Huge dataset

      ↓

Batch 1
200 records

Batch 2
200 records

Batch 3
200 records

...

Each execute chunk gets its own transaction context

That last point matters.

Governor limits are applied per batch execution transaction rather than treating the entire million-record workload as one giant transaction.


---

Scheduled Apex

Example requirement:

> Every night process stale records.



public class NightlyProcessor
    implements Schedulable {

    public void execute(
        SchedulableContext context
    ) {

        Database.executeBatch(
            new AccountBatch()
        );
    }
}

A common architecture can therefore be:

Scheduled Apex
      ↓
Batch Apex
      ↓
Process large dataset


---

Which async mechanism?

Interview scenario:

“Need to process 1 million records?”

→ Batch Apex.

“Need to perform structured asynchronous processing after a trigger?”

→ Often Queueable.

“Need it every night?”

→ Scheduled Apex, potentially launching Batch/Queueable as appropriate.

“Simple existing async method using primitive parameters?”

→ Future may be encountered, although Queueable is generally preferable for new structured async work.

Don't answer every asynchronous question with Queueable. Choose based on requirement.


---

Async Apex testing

Queueable:

@IsTest
private class AccountProcessorTest {

    @IsTest
    static void testQueueable() {

        Account acc =
            new Account(Name = 'Test');

        insert acc;

        Set<Id> ids =
            new Set<Id>{acc.Id};

        Test.startTest();

        System.enqueueJob(
            new AccountProcessor(ids)
        );

        Test.stopTest();

        // assertions
    }
}

Why Test.stopTest() matters here?

Because asynchronous work queued between startTest() and stopTest() is executed synchronously for the test by stopTest(), allowing you to assert its outcome.


---

Callout testing

Real HTTP callout should not be made from a unit test.

Use:

HttpCalloutMock

Example:

@IsTest
global class OpportunityMock
    implements HttpCalloutMock {

    global HTTPResponse respond(
        HTTPRequest request
    ) {

        HttpResponse response =
            new HttpResponse();

        response.setStatusCode(200);

        response.setBody(
            '{"status":"success"}'
        );

        return response;
    }
}

Test:

Test.setMock(
    HttpCalloutMock.class,
    new OpportunityMock()
);

Then enqueue/run the functionality.

This is very interview-worthy because REST integration + testing often get asked together.

