2. Core Java — Exception Handling | ~25 min

Consider:

int result = 10 / 0;

This causes:

ArithmeticException

Handle:

try {

    int result = 10 / 0;

} catch (ArithmeticException e) {

    System.out.println(
        "Cannot divide by zero"
    );
}

finally

try {

    // risky operation

} catch (Exception e) {

    // handle error

} finally {

    // cleanup
}

finally is typically used for cleanup that should occur whether the operation succeeds or fails, subject to exceptional JVM termination scenarios.


---

Checked vs unchecked exceptions

Java interview staple.

Checked exception

Compiler requires handling/declaration.

Example:

IOException

Unchecked exception

Subclass of:

RuntimeException

Examples:

NullPointerException
ArithmeticException
IllegalArgumentException


---

throw vs throws

throw actually throws an exception:

if (age < 18) {
    throw new IllegalArgumentException(
        "Age must be 18+"
    );
}

throws declares that a method may propagate one:

public void readFile()
    throws IOException {

    // ...
}

Remember:

throw
→ actually throw exception

throws
→ declare possible exception


---

Custom exception

class InvalidEmployeeException
    extends Exception {

    public InvalidEmployeeException(
        String message
    ) {
        super(message);
    }
}

Use:

if (employee == null) {

    throw new InvalidEmployeeException(
        "Employee cannot be null"
    );
}

Java interview drill

Explain:

checked vs unchecked exception

throw vs throws

try/catch/finally

why swallowing exceptions is dangerous


Bad:

try {
    process();
} catch (Exception e) {
}

Why bad?

Because the error disappears with no logging, handling or propagation.


